MEMORY FORENSICS: IDENTIFY BEFORE READING

The RP2040 has Flash XIP code and SRAM for kernel state and users.
Pico W commonly reserves six 24-KiB user slots; each slot also
contains a reserved 4-KiB stack. Verify actual layout per build.
Never infer a live PID's slot from its PID number.

READ-ONLY START
  ps
  cat /proc/memory
  cat /proc/ramfiles
  lsof
  ls /proc/sym
  grep pwm /proc/sym/kernel
  mem -s kernel

BOUNDED READS
  hexdump /dev/shm 0 64
  hexdump /tmp/a.out 0 64
  hexdump /dev/mem 0x2000e000 32
Last address is the FIRST slot's start in an example Pico W map,
NOT the automatically determined address of a particular task.
Confirm allocation using the current memory report before probing.

HEXDUMP FORMAT
  2000e000  01 02 ...  | ...
Left: byte address/offset. Middle: unsigned byte values in hex.
Right: printable ASCII only; '.' means nonprintable, not corruption.
Each output line covers up to 16 bytes. 64 source bytes usually
expand to several hundred text bytes. MCP output is capped at 4 KiB.
An all-zero area does not prove a free region, and an ASCII string
does not prove a valid pointer or object.

REPEATABILITY
  Capture a short range twice, comparing specific offsets.
  If bytes change, consider counters, stack, concurrent task I/O or
  live buffers before alleging memory corruption.
  Never write /dev/mem as a diagnostic shortcut. Unsafe reads may
  fault if a range is invalid or inaccessible.
